          "answer": "RUN_ID=alpha0\n\n# Investigation Checklist: Python HTTP Memory Leak\n\nThis checklist provides a systematic approach to diagnosing and resolving memory leaks in Python-based HTTP servers (e.g., Flask, Django, FastAPI, Tornado). It covers environment setup, monitoring, profiling, code inspection, and verification.\n\n## Phase 1: Environment & Baseline Setup\n\n1. **Record Initial Memory Footprint**\n   - *Command:* `python3 -c \"import psutil; p = psutil.Process(); print(f'Initial RSS: {p.memory_info().rss / 1024 / 1024:.2f} MB')\"`\n   - *Verification:* Note the baseline RSS (Resident Set Size) in MB. This is your reference point for measuring growth.\n\n2. **Confirm Python Version and GC Settings**\n   - *Command:* `python3 --version` and `python3 -c \"import gc; print(gc.isenabled(), gc.get_threshold())\"`\n   - *Verification:* Ensure you know the exact Python version (e.g., 3.10, 3.11) as GC behavior changes between versions. Note current GC thresholds.\n\n3. **Enable Detailed Logging for HTTP Requests**\n   - *Action:* Add middleware or logging to log every incoming request with a unique ID and timestamp.\n   - *Verification:* Confirm logs capture request start/end times and response status codes. This helps correlate memory spikes with specific requests.\n\n4. **Set Up Continuous Memory Monitoring**\n   - *Command:* `while true; do python3 -c \"import psutil; p = psutil.Process(); print(f'RSS: {p.memory_info().rss / 1024 / 1024:.2f} MB')\"; sleep 5; done > mem_log.txt &",
